# Response headers for Cloudflare Pages.
#
# INERT ON GITHUB PAGES — it serves this as a plain file and ignores it. It
# takes effect the moment Cloudflare Pages serves the site, so it is committed
# ahead of the move.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. GitHub Pages sent a flat `Cache-Control: max-age=600` for
# everything it served, sw.js included. A ten-minute browser cache in FRONT of
# the service worker is the stale-code trap this repo's sw.js header comments
# describe from the other side: the worker's CACHE constant is supposed to be
# the single thing deciding what a returning phone runs, and an HTTP cache the
# app cannot see gets a vote it should not have.
#
# So: the service worker owns caching. The network layer revalidates and stays
# out of its way.

/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

# The worker itself must never be held. A stale sw.js cannot be fixed by
# bumping CACHE, because the file that carries the new CACHE is the stale one.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate

# Artwork is the exception. `no-cache` still costs a conditional request per
# file, and on an Egyptian mobile connection forty 304s is seconds of latency
# for artwork that did not change. A day, revalidated after that — which keeps
# the house rule working: replace a render and its FILENAME changes, or the
# change is not guaranteed to reach a phone that already has the old one.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, must-revalidate
